      <Xref identifier="Q9FJI1" dataSource="uniprot" />
      <Graphics centerX="150" centerY="555" width="90.0" height="25.0" textColor="000000" fontName="Arial" fontWeight="Normal" fontStyle="Normal" fontDecoration="Normal" fontStrikethru="Normal" fontSize="12" hAlign="Center" vAlign="Middle" borderColor="000000" borderStyle="Solid" borderWidth="1.0" fillColor="FFFFFF" shapeType="Rectangle" />
      <Comment source="pmngroup">Adenylate kinase (AK) is ubiquitous in the cells of all living organisms and plays a crucial role in adenine nucleotide metabolism, governing the equilibrium of adenylates &lt;i&gt;in vivo&lt;/i&gt;. In plants the enzyme&apos;s activity has profound consequences for photosynthetic |CITS: [11696011]| and respiratory metabolism |CITS: [14670601]|, as well as primary metabolism under low oxygen conditions (hypoxia and anoxia) |CITS: [21749322]|. Various isoforms of AK were found in different subcellular locations and with various functions in a wide range of plant species, such as |FRAME: TAX-4577 &lt;i&gt;Zea mays&lt;/i&gt;| |CITS: [16664952]|, |FRAME: TAX-4113 &lt;i&gt;Solanum tuberosum&lt;/i&gt;| |CITS: [18252705]|, and |FRAME: TAX-4530 &lt;i&gt;Oryza sativa&lt;/i&gt;| |CITS: [7766884]|. The |FRAME: TAX-3702 &lt;i&gt;Arabidopsis thaliana&lt;/i&gt;| genome contains 10 genes with an adenylate/cytidylate kinase signature, eight of which identified as putative adenylate kinases (AMK1 through AMK7 and AAK6). At least two members (AMK2 and AMK5) of this gene family are targeted to plastids, AMK3 and AMK4 are cytosolic, AMK7 is mitochondrial |CITS: [18162585]| and AAK6 is located in the nucleus |CITS: [23121860]|. The location of AMK1 is disputed. In one report a reduced adenylate kinase activity was observe in isolated chloroplasts from mutants with disruption of AMK1, leading to the conclusion that AMK1 is located in plastids |CITS: [15618410]|. However, later work using a stably transformed GFP-fusion with AMK1 revealed mitochondrial localization |CITS: [18162585]|. It is possible that this enzyme has dual targeting. Expression analysis found that AMK1 to AMK5 are much higher expressed than AMK6, and that AMK7 is expressed at the detection limit. Expression of AMK2 and AMK5 was positively correlated with the expression of genes involved in photosynthesis and major carbohydrate metabolism, and the expression of AMK3, AMK4, and AMK1 was negatively correlated with genes of photosynthesis and uncorrelated with genes of carbohydrate metabolism. A significant positive correlation was found for the expression of AMK1 with genes of light-independent energy metabolism |CITS: [18162585]|. The AAK6 gene has been cloned and expressed in |FRAME:TAX-562 &lt;i&gt;Escherichia coli&lt;/i&gt;|, and the recombinant protein was purified and characterized |CITS: [23121860]|. The enzyme has characteristic adenylate kinase properties, with ATP as the preferred phosphate donor and AMP as the best phosphate acceptor, although the enzyme can phosporylate a few other nucleotides, including dAMP. AAK6 T-DNA insertion mutants had decreased stem growth compared with wild-type plants, suggesting it is an essential growth factor in Arabidopsis |CITS: [23121860]|.</Comment>
